From 9f9818d7f76dca48a0bbef4dcd2a01f6e427f313 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Artem Butusov <art.sormy@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 7 Sep 2026 00:08:27 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] skw: do not lose the suspend handshake to a single-sample
 race

send_host_suspend_indication() tells the chip the host is going down by
driving gpio_out low and waiting for an ack, but it only does so if
gpio_in reads low first. gpio_in is the chip's "I have traffic for you"
line, and the suspend path generates traffic of its own - the Bluetooth
disconnect the core performs on the way into suspend, anything still in
flight - so the one sample it takes is exactly the moment the line is
most likely to be high.

Measured on an H96 Max 3518D: the line is low 88% of the time while idle
(177 of 200 samples at 100 ms), yet it read high at every one of five
consecutive suspends. Each time the handshake was skipped, the chip was
never told the host had gone down, and it went on asserting gpio_in until
the wake-enabled interrupt resumed the box - typically within a few
seconds, and with no HCI event to show for it, because nothing had
happened at the Bluetooth layer at all.

Wait for the line instead of sampling it, bounded by a 200 ms jiffies
deadline (an msleep(1) loop counter would be 200 ticks, ~1.6 s at HZ=250). If the chip
really is busy the wait expires and the function falls through exactly as
before, so a genuinely active chip behaves no differently.

The same suspend path also leaves suspend_wake_unlock_enable clear. Only
skw_sdio_irq_ops(1) sets it, and the caller that would reach it gives up
first when gpio_in is high, so every GPIO interrupt taken while suspended
grabs a wakelock through skw_sdio_lock_rx_ws() - meaning even a spurious
wake then holds the system awake rather than letting it go back down. Set
it as skw_sdio_suspend() returns, and only when it returns success:
skw_sdio_resume() is what clears it again, and the PM core does not resume
a device whose suspend callback failed.
---
 drivers/seekwaveplatform_lite/sdio/skw_sdio_main.c |  8 ++++++++
 drivers/seekwaveplatform_lite/sdio/skw_sdio_rx.c   | 10 ++++++++++
 2 files changed, 18 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/seekwaveplatform_lite/sdio/skw_sdio_main.c b/drivers/seekwaveplatform_lite/sdio/skw_sdio_main.c
index b3a76fb..6025fbe 100644
--- a/drivers/seekwaveplatform_lite/sdio/skw_sdio_main.c
+++ b/drivers/seekwaveplatform_lite/sdio/skw_sdio_main.c
@@ -882,6 +882,14 @@ static int skw_sdio_suspend(struct device *dev)
 		skw_sdio->power_off = 1;
 #endif
 	skw_sdio->resume_com = 0;
+	/* Only skw_sdio_irq_ops(1) sets this, and the suspend path never gets there - its caller
+	 * bails out when gpio_in is high. Left clear, every GPIO interrupt taken while suspended
+	 * grabs a wakelock, so even a spurious wake then holds the system up instead of letting
+	 * it go back down. Set it only on the way out clean: resume is what clears it, and the
+	 * PM core does not resume a device whose suspend returned an error.
+	 */
+	if (!ret)
+		skw_sdio->suspend_wake_unlock_enable = 1;
 	return ret;
 }
 
diff --git a/drivers/seekwaveplatform_lite/sdio/skw_sdio_rx.c b/drivers/seekwaveplatform_lite/sdio/skw_sdio_rx.c
index 62e0098..11af03c 100644
--- a/drivers/seekwaveplatform_lite/sdio/skw_sdio_rx.c
+++ b/drivers/seekwaveplatform_lite/sdio/skw_sdio_rx.c
@@ -1718,9 +1718,19 @@ void send_host_suspend_indication(struct skw_sdio_data_t *skw_sdio)
 {
 	uint32_t value = 0;
 	uint32_t timeout = 2000, timeout1 = 20;
+	unsigned long deadline;
 	if(skw_sdio->gpio_out>=0 && skw_sdio->gpio_in>=0 && skw_sdio->resume_com) {
 		skw_sdio_dbg("%s enter gpio=0\n", __func__);
 		skw_sdio->host_active = 0;
+		/* The chip raises gpio_in whenever it has traffic for us, and suspend generates some
+		 * of its own - the Bluetooth disconnect, anything still in flight. Sampling the line
+		 * once therefore loses a race it would otherwise win: measured low 88% of the time
+		 * while idle, yet high at every single suspend. Lose it and the handshake below is
+		 * skipped, the chip is never told the host went down, and it keeps waking it.
+		 */
+		deadline = jiffies + msecs_to_jiffies(200);
+		while (gpio_get_value(skw_sdio->gpio_in) && time_before(jiffies, deadline))
+			msleep(1);
 		if (gpio_get_value(skw_sdio->gpio_in) == 0) {
 			udelay(10);
 			if (gpio_get_value(skw_sdio->gpio_in) == 0) {
-- 
